94139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078365 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} & =\\frac{3\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{0+1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1} \n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(3x)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{x + 1} - 1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2-1^2}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x+1-1}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 0}3(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)\\\n&=3(\\sqrt{0+1}+1)\\\n\t&=6.\n}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186078372 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186078372 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}$?\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\,? \\,$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078372 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4$, we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 7x - 2y \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 1 - 2 \\cdot (-4) \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078372 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186078380 1071 "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078380 1071 "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186078380 1074 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0$.\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50)\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12$, and $a=50$. Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078380 1074 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186078383 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186078383 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078383 1074 "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2y^{2} - x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{2} - 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 18 - 1 \\\\\n&= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 17 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx \\\\\n&= 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 3 + 17 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 20\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078383 1074 "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186078384 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} .$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186078384 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078384 1074 "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both the numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)\\frac{2\\sin x}{3x} &= lim_(x → 0)(\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sin x}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186078384 1074 "Notice that as $x \\to 0,$ both the numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186083116 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x) =x^3 - x^2 - 4x + 4$ has a root in the interval $(0,4)$, find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186083116 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x) =x^3 - x^2 - 4x + 4$ has a root in the interval $(0,4),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186083116 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x)$, the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $1$. So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of}{4}}{\\textrm{factor of}1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n$\\pm1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,4)$, are $1$ and $2$.\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether\n\n$$\n\\left(x-1\\right)\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x)$. We get the following:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(x-1\\right)\n$$\n\nis indeed a factor of $f(x)$. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\left(x - 1\\right) (x^2 - 4)\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (x - 1)(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0$, we find the roots $x=-2, 1, 2$. Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+1+2 =1\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186083116 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{4}}{\\textrm{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,4),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\left( x - 1\\right) (x^2 - 4).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2, 1, 2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+1+2 =1.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186083117 1071 "Calculate the value of ${}_{11}P_2$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186083117 1071 "Calculate the value of ${}_{11}P_2.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186083117 1074 "The formula for ${}_nP_r$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r  = \\dfrac {n!} {(n - r)!} \\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n={11}$ and $r=2$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n        \\eqalign{\n        \t\n        \t{}_{11}P_2  &= \\dfrac {11!} {(11 - 2)!} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {11!}{9!} \\[5pt]\n                            &= \\dfrac {11 \\cdot 10 \\cdot  \\cancel{9!}} {\\cancel{9!}} \\[5pt]\n                            &= 11 \\cdot 10 \\[5pt]\n                            &= 110.\n         }\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186083117 1074 "The formula for ${}_nP_r$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r = \\dfrac {n!} {(n - r)!} \\,\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n={11}$ and $r=2$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} {}_{11}P_2 &= \\dfrac {11!} {(11 - 2)!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {11!}{9!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {11 \\cdot 10 \\cdot \\cancel{9!}} {\\cancel{9!}} \\\\[5pt] &= 11 \\cdot 10 \\\\[5pt] &= 110. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186083118 1071 "Evaluate $(12; 10)$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186083118 1071 "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned})$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186083118 1074 "First, note that $\\displaystyle{{12}\\choose{10}}$ means the same thing as $_{12}C_{10}$.\n\nIn general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}\n$$\n\n