194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115136 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overline{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =5+3+a+a+a+7 \\\\  & =15+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (15+3a) \\\\  & =\\frac{15+3a}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overline{x} = 9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =\\frac{15+3a}{6} \\\\ 54 & =15+3a \\\\ 39 & =3a \\\\ a & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115139 1071 "What is the variance of the following data set?\n![](images/af/af19f9dd9e0d806dc6c036d0c8e7097fc8472b224dda61a81918798daf6d882d.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115139 1071 "What is the variance of the following data set?\n$5, \\: 13, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: 3$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115139 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ![](images/61/6195d5c7fc81945eef3b519190110b37ed9e0863254ac2275595901ebaffe604.png) then the variance of the data set is given by \n![](images/1c/1ceec547dfbb58166bb3522d24a12f3eba912db905aaa012ad6c73f03fdd9512.png)\nwhere ![](images/b5/b5d1d41f5aa6f27293caa6de256b26ff99a3c0aaf1d89d366f5f2dca8dfa53a2.png) is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are ![](images/b3/b3ed24ffd49ecbb6d34459118c56c6426d20d7668736aec9217a606c95d50734.png) points in our data set, so the mean is\n![](images/fb/fb443a611b9826f4ea13a518a529a258dde739c4c38a257a503feb6185afbe35.png)\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n![](images/f4/f430fa46c44c68395d19f47d7c227933fd08b9df5648a22d1e14aa168bab9560.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115139 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the variance of the data set is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nis the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot (5 + 13 + 6 + 8 + 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 35 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}[(5 - 7)^{2} + (13 - 7)^{2} + (6 - 7)^{2} + (8 - 7)^{2} + (3 - 7)^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}[4 + 36 + 1 + 1 + 16] \\\\\n&= \\frac{58}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115140 1071 "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n4, \\: 5, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 8, \\: 11, \\: 3\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115140 1071 "What is the variance of the following data set?\n$4, \\: 5, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 8, \\: 11, \\: 3$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115140 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}$. There are $n=7$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot (4 + 5 + 4 + 7 + 8 + 11 + 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot 42 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}$. Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot (4^{2} + 5^{2} + 4^{2} + 7^{2} + 8^{2} + 11^{2} + 3^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot (16 + 25 + 16 + 49 + 64 + 121 + 9) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7} \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= \\frac{300}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{300}{7} - (6)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{300}{7} - 36 \\\\\n&= \\frac{300 - 252}{7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{48}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115140 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}.$ There are $n=7$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (4+5+4+7+8+11+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 42 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot ({4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (16+25+16+49+64+121+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 300 \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{7}-{(6)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{7}-36 \\\\  & =\\frac{300-252}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115141 1071 "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n\n$$\n7, \\: 5, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 1, \\: 3\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115141 1071 "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n$7, \\: 5, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 1, \\: 3$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115141 1074 "The standard deviation of a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{ \\sigma^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\,\\,\\, \\vphantom{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n} \\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 }\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}.$ There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (7+5+0+2+1+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot ({7}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (49+25+0+4+1+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{88}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{3}-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{3}-9 \\\\  & =\\frac{44-27}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{ \\sigma^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{17}{3} }.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115141 1074 "The standard deviation of a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2} = \\sqrt{\\,\\,\\, \\vphantom{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n} \\overline{x^2} - \\left(\\overline{x} \\right)^2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}$. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (7 + 5 + 0 + 2 + 1 + 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 18 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}$. Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (7^{2} + 5^{2} + 0^{2} + 2^{2} + 1^{2} + 3^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (49 + 25 + 0 + 4 + 1 + 9) \\\\\n&= \\frac{88}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{44}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{44}{3} - (3)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{44}{3} - 9 \\\\\n&= \\frac{44 - 27}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{17}{3}}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115142 1071 "Compute the variance $\\sigma^2$ of a data set $x_i$ consisting of $8$ data points, where\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115142 1071 "Compute the variance $\\sigma^2$ of a data set $x_i$ consisting of $8$ data points, where\n\n$\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115142 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, then the variance of the data set is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}$. There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40\n$$\n\nso we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}∑_(i = 1)^(8)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}$. We know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320\n$$\n\nso we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}^(―) &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}∑_(i = 1)^(8)x_{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 320 \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^{2} &= x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= 40 - (5)^{2} \\\\\n&= 40 - 25 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186115142 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}.$ We know that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 320 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =40-{(5)}^{2} \\\\  & =40-25 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186115143 1071 "The inverse of the $3 \\times 3$ matrix $K$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $K^{-1}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}K & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 