85 true] [17592186124406 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P$, the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n$1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij}$,\nand hence,\n$1\\cdot A=A$.\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P$, the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we obtain that\n$\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij}$,\nand hence,\n$\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$.\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P$, the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we obtain that\n$(\\alpha+\\beta) a_{ij} = \\alpha a_{ij} + \\beta a_{ij}$,\nand hence,\n$(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124406 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) a_{ij} = \\alpha a_{ij} + \\beta a_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124407 1071 "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$, and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n2. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n4. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124407 1071 "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R},$ and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124407 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N$, then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0$. Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0$. This matrix belongs to $N$, and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N$, then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0$. If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0$. This matrix does not belong to $N$, and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124407 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0.$ Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0.$ This matrix belongs to $N,$  and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N,$ then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0.$ If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0.$ This matrix does not belong to $N,$  and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124408 1071 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3$, and its subset $A = \\big\\{at^3-bt \\: \\mid \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n2. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n4. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124408 1071 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{ at^3-bt \\: | \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124408 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A$, then\n$f(t) + g(t)|= (a_{1}t^{3} - b_{1}t) + (a_{2}t^{3} - b_{2}t); = (a_{1} + a_{2})t^{3} - (b_{1} + b_{2})t$,\nwhich is a polynomial from $A$. So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then\n$kf(t)|= k(at^{3} - bt); = (ka)t^{3} - (kb)t$,\nwhich is a polynomial from $A$. So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124408 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{3}-{b}_{1}t)+({a}_{2}{t}^{3}-{b}_{2}t) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{3}-({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{3}-bt) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{3}-(kb)t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124409 1071 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\big\\{0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n2. $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n3. $\\big\\{0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124409 1071 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\big\\{ 0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n\n- $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n\n- $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124409 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n$1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0$,\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}$.\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124409 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124410 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124410 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124410 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(r(t)) &= (2\\cos t - 1)i + (2\\sin t - 1)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti + (-\\sin t)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= ∇f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= (2\\cos t - 1i + (2\\sin t - 1)j) \\cdot (\\cos ti + (-\\sin t)j) \\\\\n&= (2\\cos t - 1) \\cdot \\cos t + (2\\sin t - 1) \\cdot (-\\sin t) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos^{2} t - \\cos t - 2\\sin^{2} t + \\sin t \\\\\n&= 2(\\cos^{2} t - \\sin^{2} t) + \\sin t - \\cos t \\\\\n&= 2\\cos 2t + \\sin t - \\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(π))] &= 2\\cos 2π + \\sin π - \\cos π \\\\\n&= 2(1) + 0 - (-1) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124410 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124411 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124411 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124411 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta$, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂θ} &= \\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂θ} + \\frac{∂f}{∂y} \\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\sin θ + \\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = x, \\frac{∂f}{∂y} = 2y\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= r\\cos θ, \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= 2r\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂θ} &= - (r\\cos θ)r\\sin θ + (2r\\sin θ)r\\cos θ \\\\\n&=-r^{2}\\sin θ\\cos θ + 2r^{2}\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= r^{2}\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}r^{2}\\sin (2θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124411 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124412 1071 "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124412 1071 "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124412 1074 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124412 1074 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂r} = \\cos 3θ, \\frac{∂w}{∂θ} =-3r\\sin 3θ\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂w}{∂x} &= (\\cos 3θ)\\cos θ - \\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\sin 3θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ\\cos 3θ + 3\\sin θ\\sin 3θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124413 1071 "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2)$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}$, where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124413 1071 "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2),$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124413 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta$, and $z$, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nand $z=z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂w}{∂θ} &= \\frac{∂w}{∂x} \\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂θ} + \\frac{∂w}{∂y} \\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂θ} + \\frac{∂w}{∂z} \\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\sin θ + \\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂x} = 2xz^{3}, \\frac{∂w}{∂y} = 4yz^{3}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta$, and $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂w}{∂x} &= 2rz^{3}\\cos θ, \\\\\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y} &= 4rz^{3}\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂w}{∂θ} &= - (2rz^{3}\\cos θ)r\\sin θ + (4rz^{3}\\sin θ)r\\cos θ \\\\\n&=-2r^{2}z^{3}\\cos θ\\sin θ + 4r^{2}z^{3}\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2r^{2}z^{3}\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= r^{2}z^{3}\\sin 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124413 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=2x{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124414 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124414 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124414 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)f(x) &= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{x^{5} - 10}{x^{3} - 8x^{2}} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → ∞)((\\frac{x^{5}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}}))/((\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{8x^{2}}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → ∞)((x^{2} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}}))/((1 - \\frac{8}{x})) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{x^{2} - 0}{1 - 0}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → ∞)x^{2} \\\\\n&= ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124414 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124415 1071 "Identify the surface $-x^2 + z^2 + 2x + y=0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124415 1071 "Identify the surface $-x^2 + z^2 + 2x + y=0.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124415 1074 "First, we complete the square in the $x$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + z^{2} + 2x + y &= 0 \\\\\n- (x^{2} - 2x) + z^{2} + y &= 0 \\\\\n- (x - 1)^{2} + 1 + z^{2} + y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (x - 1)^{2} + 1 + z^{2} + y &= 0 \\\\\n- (x - 1)^{2} + z^{2} &= -y - 1 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - z^{2} &= y + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(1, -1, 0)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124415 1074 "First, we complete the square in the $x$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2x+y & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-2x)+{z}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)^{2}+1+{z}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x-1)^{2}+1+{z}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-y-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{z}^{2} & =y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(1, -1, 0).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124416 1071 "![](images/bc/bc31c5e4a0e362c51b23718aa121b064b72cc6b7dd9965f6d67f7ce83776c440.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PDP^{-1},$ what is the matrix $P?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124416 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PDP^{-1}$, what is the matrix $P$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124416 1074 "Notice that $A=PDP^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A$. So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A$.\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4 \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\lambda_3=0\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=4$. Then,\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-\\frac{1}{2})R_{1}; R_{3}:=R_{3} + \\frac{1}{2}R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2+3x_3$. Hence, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} + 3x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Consider $\\lambda=0$. Then,\n$A - 0I|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} - 0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$, we obtain the eigenvector $\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124416 1074 "Notice that $A=PDP^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_3=0.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=4.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2+3x_3.$ Hence, the general solution is\n![](images/8e/8e827da643179ea7b1943c875f936de99bbc9a3a01ba35fcb8fb2095e2beac80.png)\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/6a/6ad3b3b6b8db1e15fb26a6eec89d02c886ec91a2c7bf675980d8a8f638805cb3.png) and ![](images/86/86e7ef62c868c3456422c0d6c5e4dcfdfafba566b1c76af2a92a90ec71ca1147.png)\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/38/38ddc9b0ce63866264f198b968f61ae7ef60174510c0badbbcc35019ecb42d0a.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n![](images/22/2258c5f9447a21fdc3d9d913aab67bb6f725bc67abdec8076d0ca0d9825232d9.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124417 1071 "Determine if the matrix\n![](images/df/dfc9ee546f72ad68cf116b8f67d165ba9b6ff6e77dce88f145dda93f13091441.png)\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124417 1071 "If the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124417 1074 "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal.\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3\n$$\n\nSince we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 3I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124417 1074 "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124418 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/71/7126688b8500e60258d29ab4b1b323ac956ad41b530c5723bcc71d60b11e73a0.png)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124418 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP$, find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124418 1074 "From the diagonal matrix ![](images/f3/f3460b47901f79529216c1440403e2115ff9047cb997070893b241cd938728de.png) we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/95/95d1bcb284138711bcf5afddb3f09c702e664fbdc7b256b0eaf81044f87403cf.png)\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/89/898d72a1a0cc123d1ff7ec