re stochastic matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124431 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1$. A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{bmatrix}0.35 + 0.65 = 1; 0.95 + 0.05 = 1$\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124431 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.35+0.65=1 \\\\ 0.95+0.05=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up 