x\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many two-year periods, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P$, and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that\n\n$$\nPq = q⟹(P - I)q = 0\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_{1} = 8x_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}8x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x_{2} + x_{2} &= 1 \\\\\n9x_{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx_{2} &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{9}}}\n$$\n\nof the $5\\,400$ customers would eventually own a desktop computer. Therefore, the total number of people who would eventually own a desktop computer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\cdot 5\\,400 = 600\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124433 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n![](images/19/1935c4960a48e2b446c2610b9edc2a160a5f45462494c72a90f139ea2d13cc34.png)\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many two-year periods, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](images/29/29b5a209bb27b24f165ee418326763d8fb24f83f2b3a767052c381aa8cd0e01c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=8{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](images/0e/0e8f620c3392dbb77b1353184e38eaf48b526aa82c502772a5582bd20c7f5aea.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur steady-state vector is\n![](images/2e/2ed7b0fc7296b2b651909c7c15c73e253fc8a9f95fb037072228d5de6f838894.png)\n\nSo, ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{9}}}$ of the $5\\,400$ customers would eventually own a desktop computer. Therefore, the total number of people who would eventually own a desktop computer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\cdot 5\\,400 = 600.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186124434 1071 "![](images/09/093de94431946cb83e7cc0523405989b7