0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{2}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}x_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{3}x_{3} + \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} + x_{3} &= 1 \\\\\n2x_{3} &= 1 \\\\\nx_{3} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186124434 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](images/5e/5ed2f76d9b2857134a5b2aa5781711f9625cabb46a9ca3c3d54aa31c32bb4dbe.png)\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & -0.6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=20{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=20{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+9{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-7{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 57 & -19 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2x_1 -7\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\\right) + x_3 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{2}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](images/9a/9ae5533dc825d2d3d08ca7a54c50578e21dda8fad7988b9043baaad2a69368b7.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n![](images/fd/fd7775b5d982d0bc51e80417a70b67dbff0176afcc5ff9aec613947076201386.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129028 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X \\gt 2)$.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.8$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129028 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X \\gt 2).$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.8$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129028 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X > a) = 1 - P(X\\leq a) = 1-F(a)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2) \\\\\n&= 1 - F(2) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.6 \\\\\n&= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129028 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X > a) = 1 - P(X\\leq a) = 1-F(a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-F(2) \\\\  & =1-0.6 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129030 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R$, given by\n$R = \\Big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{2y}, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq yz \\Big\\}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129030 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\Big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{2y}, \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq yz \\Big\\}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129030 1074 "Notice that we have a three-dimensional type II region.\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= ∭_(R)dV \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}\\int_{0}^{yz}dxdzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}[x]_{x = 0}^{x = yz}dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}yzdzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}y[\\frac{z^{2}}{2}]_{z = 0}^{z = \\sqrt{2y}}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2}(\\sqrt{2y})^{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}y^{2}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{y = 1}^{y = 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(8 - 1) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129030 1074 "Notice that we have a three-dimensional type II region.\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}{\\int }_{0}^{yz}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}[x]_{x=0}^{x=yz}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2y}}yz\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y{[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{2y}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2}{(\\sqrt{2y})}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(8-1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129031 1071 "![](images/04/043adf0ef683c24fbf4680145411d86fa94b52016bee44ac8f08ca7c5ca9f1b7.png)\n\n \n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129031 1071 "![](images/04/043adf0ef683c24fbf4680145411d86fa94b52016bee44ac8f08ca7c5ca9f1b7.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129031 1074 "![](images/75/75a76a2dbc34c889335e66623e12b686eb9a255ee0d67f9be0e3792f2650360c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 4-x-y \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/e1/e19ffe6a0555851c2b05526272beb21dbcdc082a23a2e11809c661666efdf133.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 4-x-y \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-x-y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x-y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(4-x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((4-x)(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6-2x\\,dx \\\\  & =[6x-{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(6(1)-{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129031 1074 "![](images/75/75a76a2dbc34c889335e66623e12b686eb9a255ee0d67f9be0e3792f2650360c.png)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 4-x-y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/e1/e19ffe6a0555851c2b05526272beb21dbcdc082a23a2e11809c661666efdf133.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 4-x-y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= ∭_(R)dV \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{4 - x - y}dzdydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}[z]_{z = 0}^{z = 4 - x - y}dydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}(4 - x - y) - 0dydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}4 - x - ydydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[(4 - x)y - \\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}((4 - x)(2) - \\frac{2^{2}}{2}) - 0dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}6 - 2xdx \\\\\n&= [6x - x^{2}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= (6(1) - 1^{2}) - 0 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129032 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the surface ![](images/95/9597cda64435e7e578c996c4c5d3e3056705e5c4fb79ccbb9bbf48cd7befe296.png) the plane ![](images/cd/cd3b605899fbd8e49350a7f9fc6184911d4308e39ec94fe5b072cf0697b131f8.png) and the planes ![](images/7e/7eb560050bef519d15d7bea27856044c51d6bdd794b40b95f2dd8a3c50e59c0f.png) ![](images/ff/ffebc693860930a5435af764f3707689c9f70fc4ad6c76