ith respect to ![](images/d2/d2a923f8537e18206a972cc41916ae6f9ee26abc93734cd0a73c50ffc165e08a.png) treating ![](images/0e/0ea8d498dfe5ba44cab2ca474bceae4ef3205271d683d0fdeedf7ffd68a22a43.png) as a constant:\n\n![](images/a9/a9b1d2210f33a1242fccf6517fbc63783381f19cdc48daf78685900497b0829f.png)\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to ![](images/73/73c3b5d4a287ad14dceb771dbd299c9effc9f3aff16c0f4b4051737ed99b36ae.png)\n\n![](images/3e/3e75cffa3638e6260cbe5493a8277a484fe594d41e20ad55588da0b2dcf21fca.png)\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is ![](images/32/327bb4cc2df638a85e6bbe52eeda604b942f097b691c0c2125d579596dfe54a8.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129033 1074 "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region $D$ with mass density\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)xy^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{0}^1 \\int_{0}^{x} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\int_{0}^{1}x[\\int_{0}^{x}y^{2}dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x[\\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x(\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - 0)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx &= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{5} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129034 1071 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129034 1071 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{(x,y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129034 1074 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}\n$$\n\nwith charge density\n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ &= ∬_(D)(4 - 3y^{2} + 2x)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A = \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}[\\int_{0}^{1}(4 - 3y^{2} + 2x)dx]dy &= \\int_{0}^{2}[4x - 3y^{2}x + x^{2}]_{0}^{1}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}[(4(1) - 3y^{2}(1) + (1)^{2}) - (4(0) - 3y^{2}(0) - (0)^{2})]dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(5 - 3y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}(5 - 3y^{2})dy &= [5y - y^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (5(2) - (2)^{3}) - (5(0) - (0)^{3}) \\\\\n&= (10 - 8) - 0 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129034 1074 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A\n= \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[4x-3{y}^{2}x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4(1)-3{y}^{2}(1)+(1)^{2})-(4(0)-3{y}^{2}(0)-(0)^{2})]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y & =[5y-{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(5(2)-(2)^{3})-(5(0)-(0)^{3}) \\\\  & =(10-8)-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129035 1071 "![](images/f0/f0db928874dba8d872cde46d1467686010f3484765b2d7e39807b5a32a131b09.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  .\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129035 1071 "![](images/f0/f0db928874dba8d872cde46d1467686010f3484765b2d7e39807b5a32a131b09.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density $\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129035 1074 "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at\n\n$$\n\\left(0,0\\right)\n$$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density\n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=9\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 9 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) &= 9 \\\\\nr^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)\\frac{4r\\cos θ}{\\sqrt{r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ}}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π}^{3π/2}\\int_{0}^{3}4r\\cos θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ[\\int_{0}^{3}rdr]dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{3}dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ(\\frac{9}{2} - 0)dθ \\\\\n&= 18\\int_{π}^{3π/2}\\cos θdθ \\\\\n&= 18[\\sin θ]_{π}^{3π/2} \\\\\n&= 18[- 1 - 0] \\\\\n&=-18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q =-18$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186129035 1074 "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at \n$\\left(0,0\\right).$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) =  \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname