rated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x-2})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-7}}{2\\sqrt{x-2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186129042 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\