(1)(-k-1) \\\\  & =0+(3k-3)+(-k-1) \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133689 1071 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$, find the value of $k$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133689 1071 "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133689 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(kt^{2} + 3t)(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(kt^{3} + 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= \\left.(\\frac{kt^{4}}{4} + t^{3})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{4} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{4} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{4} &= -1 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133689 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3t)(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{4}}{4}+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133690 1071 "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that ![](images/b8/b8ece1b03ba237390f26e8d47e40013abc221edcae144ca3a9dc233438d486a3.png) and ![](images/fe/febc66857330a11bcd7bb93952cf5b86373974f5bf87690fdcf526d4939f19ec.png) are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133690 1071 "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3$.\n\nGiven that $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$, find the value of $k$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133690 1074 "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= 3x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 2x_{3}y_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (2 \\cdot k) + 1 \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot (2 \\cdot (-1)) \\\\\n&= 6k - 2 - 4 \\\\\n&= 6k - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\