roduct $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133691 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle  =  \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133691 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2π}\\sin 2t\\cos tdt &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{2π}(\\sin (2t - t) + \\sin (2t + t))dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{2π}(\\sin t + \\sin 3t)dt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}(-\\cos t - \\frac{\\cos 3t}{3})\\right|_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[- \\cos (2π) - (\\cos (6π))/(3)] - \\frac{1}{2}[- \\cos 0 - \\frac{\\cos 0}{3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[- 1 - \\frac{1}{3}] - \\frac{1}{2}[- 1 - \\frac{1}{3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133692 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\np_1(t)=t+1, \\qquad\np_2(t)=t, \\qquad\np_3(t)=t-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ of polynomials of degree $1$ or less in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $p_2(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $S=\\{ p_1(t), p_2(t), p_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133692 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n$p_1(t)=t+1, \\qquad p_2(t)=t, \\qquad p_3(t)=t-1$.\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ of polynomials of degree $1$ or less in the variable $t$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1)$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $p_2(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S=\\{p_1(t), p_2(t), p_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133692 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given 