ext{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+1-((-1)^{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $f_1(t) \\not\\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,2t+1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2{t}^{2}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-(\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $f_2(t) \\not\\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f_1(t) \\not\\perp f_2(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133695 1071 "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)$,\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133695 1071 "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133695 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(-5t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(-5t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-8)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3+0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133695 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) = \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ &= p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1) \\\\\n&= \\left.(-5t - 3)\\right|_{0} \\cdot \\left.(t - 1)\\right|_{0} + \\left.(-5t - 3)\\right|_{1} \\cdot \\left.(t - 1)\\right|_{1} \\\\\n&= (-3) \\cdot (-1) + (-8) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 3 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨q(t), q(t)⟩ &= q(0)q(0) + q(1)q(1) \\\\\n&= \\left.(t - 1)\\right|_{0} \\cdot \\left.(t - 1)\\right|_{0} + \\left.(t - 1)\\right|_{1} \\cdot \\left.(t - 1)\\right|_{1} \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot (-1) + 0 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{q(t)}p(t) &= (⟨p(t), q(t)⟩)/(⟨q(t), q(t)⟩)q(t) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{1} \\cdot (t - 1) \\\\\n&= 3(t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133696 1071 "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$, the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t$.\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V$:\n\n$f_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133696 1071 "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133696 1074 "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1}   6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot  \\sin \\pi t = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133696 1074 "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) = \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t)\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2\n$$\n\nso let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}$. This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨g(t), f_{2}(t)⟩ &= \\int_{-1}^{1}g(t)f_{2}(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{-1}^{1}6t^{2} \\cdot \\sin (πt)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{-1}^{1}6t^{2}\\sin (πt)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1]$, which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} 6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot \\sin \\pi t = 0\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}g(t) &= proj_{f_{1}(t)}g(t) + proj_{f_{2}(t)}g(t) \\\\\n&= 2 + 0 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133697 1071 "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)$,\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133697 1071 "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133697 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(0)^{2}+q(1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(0)+(6)(1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖(4t+2)-6t‖ \\\\  & =‖-2t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩ & =(-2t+2){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2(0)+2)^{2}+(-2(1)+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d = \\sqrt{4} = 2.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133697 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{q(t)}p(t) &= (⟨p(t), q(t)⟩)/(⟨q(t), q(t)⟩)q(t) \\\\\n&= (p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1))/(q(0)^{2} + q(1)^{2}) \\cdot q(t) \\\\\n&= ((2)(0) + (6)(1))/(0^{2} + 1^{2}) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= 6t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between\n\n$$\np(t) = 4t + 2\n$$\n\nand the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= ‖p(t) - proj_{q(t)}p(t)‖ \\\\\n&= ‖(4t + 2) - 6t‖ \\\\\n&= ‖ - 2t + 2‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{⟨ - 2t + 2,-2t + 2⟩}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨ - 2t + 2,-2t + 2⟩ &= \\left.(-2t + 2)\\right|_{0} \\cdot \\left.(-2t + 2)\\right|_{0} + \\left.(-2t + 2)\\right|_{1} \\cdot \\left.(-2t + 2)\\right|_{1} \\\\\n&= (-2(0) + 2)^{2} + (-2(1) + 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} + 0^{2} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133698 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}$.\nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133698 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133698 1074 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (2x + y)) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}e^{3y}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2x + y} + 3x^{2}e^{3y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∇ \\cdot F)(1, 0) &= (2)/(2(1) + 0) + 3(1)^{2}e^{3(0)} \\\\\n&= 1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133698 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{3y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133699 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133699 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $\\left(-1,0,2\\right).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133699 1074 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncurlF &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & x^{2} & -2z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2})]i - [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j + [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\\n&= (0 - 0)i - (0 - 0)j + (2x - 1)k \\\\\n&= (2x - 1)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-1,0,2\\right)\n$$\n\nand obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(curlF)(-1, 0, 2) &= (2(-1) - 1)k =-3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133699 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n![](images/88/888b12c4f687dba1199a82d7e3a5e6897e7599ea812753423de5a23ffd756888.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & {x}^{2} & -2z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-0)j+(2x-1)k \\\\  & =(2x-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-1,0,2) & =(2(-1)-1)\\,k=-3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133700 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$, evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133700 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133700 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨1, 0, 1⟩ \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨e, 1, \\frac{1}{e}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(r(1)) - f(r(0)) \\\\\n&= f(e, 1, \\frac{1}{e}) - f(1, 0, 1) \\\\\n&= (e(1)^{2} + 2 \\cdot e \\cdot \\frac{1}{e}) - (1(0)^{2} + 2(1)(1)) \\\\\n&= (e + 2) - 2 \\\\\n&= e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133700 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and $\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,1,\\frac{1}{e})-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(e(1)^{2}+2\\cdot e\\cdot \\frac{1}{e})-(1(0)^{2}+2(1)(1)) \\\\  & =(e+2)-2 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133701 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, 1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1$.\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133701 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133701 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\nabla f\n$$\n\nSince we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= ye^{xy}, \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= xe^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) &= ⟨0, 1⟩, \\\\\nr(1) &= ⟨1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$.\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C}F \\cdot dr &= \\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= f(1, 0) - f(0, 1) \\\\\n&= e^{1 \\cdot 0} - e^{0 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= 1 - 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133701 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,0)-f(0,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot 0}-{e}^{0\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133702 1071 "![](images/d3/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133702 1071 "![](images/d3/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F$?\n\n1. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n2. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n3. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$\n4. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133702 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F\n$$\n\ndenotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F$.\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P$:\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P$.\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F < 0$ at $P$.\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F =0$ at $P$.\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0\n$$\n\neverywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P$, the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133702 1074 "Recall that $\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F <         0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F =0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133703 1071 "![](images/71/71c58bf1b0fe4259ac50633a457f6a76639c0a0c188b66cce5e0fc3c7e5b0329.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133703 1071 "![](images/71/71c58bf1b0fe4259ac50633a457f6a76639c0a0c188b66cce5e0fc3c7e5b0329.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$, shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n2. $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n3. $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n4. $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133703 1074 "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F$.\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$.\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$.\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\neverywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$.\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133703 1074 "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$ everywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133704 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2)$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n$\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133704 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133704 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a78a98750e3203760d27daaebd42852198538935e2c3777a0b8a8c85d1bb0901.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= \\cos{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= x\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} =-\\sin y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = \\sin y\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)\\cos ydx + x\\sin ydy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\sin y - (-\\sin y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2\\sin ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133704 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a78a98750e3203760d27daaebd42852198538935e2c3777a0b8a8c85d1bb0901.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= \\cos{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x\\sin{y}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133705 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$, where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0)$, $(1,2)$ and $(0,2)$, traversed counterclockwise." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133705 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133705 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a87f696b1eb42588ab69800b72d821bf47b35676fc19dcd4fe2927bbf36ba3f8.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= 4x^2y\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= 2y^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 4x^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)4x^{2}ydx + 2y^{2}dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(0 - 4x^{2})dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{2x}^{2}(-4x^{2})dydx \\\\\n&=-4\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-4\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}(2 - 2x)dx \\\\\n&=-8\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}(1 - x)dx \\\\\n&=-8\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} - x^{3}dx \\\\\n&=-8[\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{x^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-8(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&=-8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133705 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/a8/a87f696b1eb42588ab69800b72d821bf47b35676fc19dcd4fe2927bbf36ba3f8.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= 4x^2y$ and $Q(x,y)= 2y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,dx+2{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(2-2x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133706 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1)$, traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133706 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186133706 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2fa3c84359cec5f5e0750552e5a4b24b20b8dbc3d65b909aea908e1505cfb5e3.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= x\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = x\\cos y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 2x\\cos y\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)x\\sin ydx + (x^{2}\\cos y - y^{2})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(2x\\cos y - x\\cos y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x\\cos ydA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-y}^{y}x\\cos ydxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186133706 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2fa3c84359cec5f5e0750552e5a4b24b20b8dbc3d65b909aea908e1505cfb5e3.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= x\\sin{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186147388 1071 "Find the phase line of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y^2 + 2y$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186147388 1071 "Find the phase line of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y^2 + 2y.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186147388 1074 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} + 2y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y + 2) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= -2, 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0$.\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)< 0$.\n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0, \\infty)$, and negative for $y\\in (-2,0)$.\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) < 0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/69/6940ca72733614d6916831fc0316ae75ca0bb07f75082c36be3897ebf9fd55d5.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186147388 1074 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =0 \\\\ y(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find t