mit: \n\n$$\ne^x = \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{x}{n}\\right)^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-n/2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186152045 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{nx/3}}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186152045 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{nx/3}}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186152045 1074 "Recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{nx/3} &= lim_(n → ∞)[(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}]^{x/3} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}]^{x/3} \\\\\n&= e^{x/3} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt[3]{e^{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186152045 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/3} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{x/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186152046 1071 "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186152046 1071 "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186152046 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{e}^{t-4}) \\\\  & =2t-{e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4 \\, \\textrm{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =2(4)-{e}^{4-4} \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & =7\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F| = |21| = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186152046 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - e^{t - 4}) \\\\\n&= 2t - e^{t - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at\n\n$$\nt=4 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= 2(4) - e^{4 - 4} \\\\\n&= 8 - 1 \\\\\n&= 7m/s^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (7) \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}F & = & 21\\end{vmatrix} = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186152047 1071 "![](images/8d/8de3a1512666201ab8d9ab7c06385dac08acb67345273a2e07252616f666cf27.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R$. Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186152047 1071 "![](images/8d/8de3a1512666201ab8d9ab7c06385dac08acb67345273a2e07252616f666cf27.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186152047 1074 "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}$.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= 3 \\cdot m_{11} + 3 \\cdot m_{21} + m_{12} + m_{22} \\\\\n&= 3m_{11} + 3m_{21} + m_{12} + m_{22}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186152047 1074 "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =3{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157109 1071 "![](images/aa/aa730804a15decac8450255e714ca1e0697f4f2326eb615d4d035a2870a49dce.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$, where the region $D$ is shown above." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157109 1071 "![](images/aa/aa730804a15decac8450255e714ca1e0697f4f2326eb615d4d035a2870a49dce.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157109 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/12/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157109 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ncentered at\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!\n$$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $x^2+\\left(y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14$, we obtain\n$x^{2} + (y - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}|= \\frac{1}{4}; (r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}|= \\frac{1}{4}; r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ - r\\sin θ + \\frac{1}{4}|= \\frac{1}{4}; r^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) - r\\sin θ|= 0; r^{2} - r\\sin θ|= 0; r(r - \\sin θ)|= 0$.\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta$.\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we get\n$y|= \\frac{1}{2}; r\\sin θ|= \\frac{1}{2}; r|= \\frac{1}{2\\sin θ}; r|= \\frac{1}{2}\\csc θ$.\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta$. Our curves intersect at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, the entire region lies between the radial lines\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nas depicted below.\n\n![](images/12/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{2y}{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)\\frac{2r\\sin θ}{r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/4}^{π/2}\\int_{1/2\\csc θ}^{\\sin θ}2\\sin θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/4}^{π/2}2\\sin θ\\int_{1/2\\csc θ}^{\\sin θ}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/4}^{π/2}2\\sin θ[r]_{1/2\\csc θ}^{\\sin θ}dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/4}^{π/2}2\\sin θ(\\sin θ - \\frac{1}{2}\\csc θ)dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/4}^{π/2}(2\\sin^{2} θ - 1)dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/4}^{π/2} - \\cos (2θ)dθ \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\sin (2θ)]_{π/4}^{π/2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(0 - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157110 1071 "![](images/94/940f547f886f1b680cbb37ef950438e6a4f33bd3c84df28a5e00480631c97711.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between the quarter-circles of radii $3$ and $4$, as shown above. What is\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157110 1071 "![](images/94/940f547f886f1b680cbb37ef950438e6a4f33bd3c84df28a5e00480631c97711.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between the quarter-circles of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157110 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157110 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{1}{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)(1)/((r\\sin θ)^{2} + (r\\cos θ)^{2})rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\int_{3}^{4}(r)/(r^{2}(\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ))drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\int_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}drdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157113 1071 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/1a/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157113 1071 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/1a/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157113 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5)$, and passes through the point $(-2,3)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-4,5)\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3)$, we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{2 = 2pt; - 2 = pt^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 2pt \\\\\n1 &= pt \\\\\nt &= \\frac{1}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2 &= p(\\frac{1}{p})^{2} \\\\\n- 2 &= \\frac{1}{p} \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157113 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157114 1071 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$, generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/83/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157114 1071 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/83/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157114 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2)$, and passes through the point $(1,0)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-3,2)\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0)$, we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{4 = pt^{2}; - 2 = 2pt\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2 &= 2pt \\\\\nt &= -\\frac{1}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= p(-\\frac{1}{p})^{2} \\\\\n4 &= p \\cdot \\frac{1}{p^{2}} \\\\\n4 &= \\frac{1}{p} \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157114 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157115 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157115 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157115 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= -\\frac{x - 1}{2}, t =-\\frac{y + 1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= (-\\frac{y + 1}{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= ((y + 1)^{2})/(16)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}\n$$\n\nand rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((y + 1)^{2})/(16) &= -\\frac{x - 1}{2} \\\\\n(y + 1)^{2} &= 16 \\cdot (-\\frac{x - 1}{2}) \\\\\n(y + 1)^{2} &= -8(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157115 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157124 1071 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n\n- The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n\n- The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157124 1071 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n1. The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n2. The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n3. The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157124 1074 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the die is rolled before $4$ appears for the first time can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, but the number of times that $2$ appears cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Tossing a coin is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the coin is tossed before the first head appears can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157124 1074 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the die is rolled before $4$ appears for the first time can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, but the number of times that $2$ appears cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Tossing a coin is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the coin is tossed before the first head appears can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157125 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shots until the archer misses. Since the chance of missing each shot is $7\\%$, the number of shots until the archer misses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 7\\% = \\dfrac{7}{100} = 0.07\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.07)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.07)^{x - 1}(0.07) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{x - 1}(0.07)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 8) &= f(8) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{8 - 1}(0.07) \\\\\n&= (0.93)^{7}(0.07) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.042\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157125 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shots until the archer misses. Since the chance of missing each shot is $7\\%,$ the number of shots  until the archer misses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 7\\% = \\dfrac{7}{100} = 0.07.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.07).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.07)^{x-1}(0.07) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{x-1}(0.07)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=8) & =f(8) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{8-1}(0.07) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{7}(0.07) \\\\  & ≈0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157126 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%$, the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.1)^{x - 1}(0.1) \\\\\n&= (0.9)^{x - 1}(0.1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) + f(3) \\\\\n&= (0.9)^{1 - 1}(0.1) + (0.9)^{2 - 1}(0.1) + (0.9)^{3 - 1}(0.1) \\\\\n&= (0.1) + (0.9)(0.1) + (0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\\n&= [1 + 0.9 + (0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.271\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157126 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%,$ the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.1)^{x-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{x-1}(0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{1-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{2-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{3-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.1)+(0.9)(0.1)+(0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =[1+0.9+(0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.271\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157127 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%$, the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35)$. In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - 0.35)^{x - 1}(0.35) \\\\\n&= (0.65)^{x - 1}(0.35)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3)$, we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) + f(3) \\\\\n&= (0.65)^{1 - 1}(0.35) + (0.65)^{2 - 1}(0.35) + (0.65)^{3 - 1}(0.35) \\\\\n&= (0.35) + (0.65)(0.35) + (0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\\n&= [1 + 0.65 + (0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.725\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 3) &= 1 - P(X \\le 3) \\\\\n&\\approx 1 - 0.725 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.275\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157127 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%,$ the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq  3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.725 \\\\  & ≈0.275\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157135 1071 "Solve the inequality $2(x - 2)^3 - 5 \\gt      11.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157135 1071 "Solve the inequality $2(x - 2)^3 - 5 \\gt 11$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157135 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-2)^{3}-5 & >11 \\\\ 2(x-2)^{3} & >16 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & >8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the cube root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(x-2)^{3}} & >\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ x-2 & >2 \\\\ x & >4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157135 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2(x - 2)^{3} - 5 & > 11 \\\\ 2(x - 2)^{3} & > 16 \\\\ (x - 2)^{3} & > 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the cube root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[3]{(x - 2)^{3}} & > \\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ x - 2 & > 2 \\\\ x & > 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157136 1071 "Solve $(5x-1)^6 + 5 \\geq -4$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157136 1071 "Solve $(5x-1)^6 + 5 \\geq -4.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157136 1074 "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-1)^{6}+5 & \\ge -4 \\\\ (5x-1)^{6} & \\ge -9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $(5x - 1)^{6} \\geq 0$ for all values of $x$ because the power $(6)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the given inequality is all real numbers." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157136 1074 "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(5x - 1)^{6} + 5 & \\ge - 4 \\\\ (5x - 1)^{6} & \\ge - 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n(5x - 1)^{6} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x$ because the power $(6)$ is even.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the given inequality is all real numbers." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157137 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{(5x + 2)^4}{9} - 3 \\gt 6$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157137 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{(5x + 2)^4}{9} - 3 \\gt 6.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157137 1074 "Isolating the quartic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(5x+2)^{4}}{9}-3 & >6 \\\\ \\frac{(5x+2)^{4}}{9} & >9 \\\\ (5x+2)^{4} & >81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the fourth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{(5x+2)^{4}} & >\\sqrt[4]{81} \\\\ |5x+2| & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n5x + 2 \\lt -3 \\qquad \\textrm{or} \\qquad 5x + 2 \\gt 3\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & <-3 &  &  & 5x+2 & >3 \\\\ 5x & <-5 &  &  & 5x & >1 \\\\ x & <-1 &  &  & x & >\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\lt -1 \\quad \\textrm{or} \\quad x \\gt \\dfrac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157137 1074 "Isolating the quartic term, we find\n$\\begin{bmatrix}((5x + 2)^{4})/(9) - 3 & > 6 \\\\ ((5x + 2)^{4})/(9) & > 9 \\\\ (5x + 2)^{4} & > 81\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we take the fourth root of both sides and get\n$\\sqrt[4]{(5x + 2)^{4}} \\mid > \\sqrt[4]{81}; \\mid 5x + 2 \\mid \\mid > 3$.\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n$5x + 2 \\lt -3 \\qquad \\textrm{or} \\qquad 5x + 2 \\gt 3$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5x + 2 & < - 3 & 5x + 2 & > 3 \\\\ 5x & < - 5 & 5x & > 1 \\\\ x & < - 1 & x & > \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\lt -1 \\quad \\textrm{or} \\quad x \\gt \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157138 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{x+3} + 1 \\geq  0.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157138 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt[3]{x+3} + 1 \\geq 0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157138 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+3}+1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+3} & \\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{x+3})}^{3} & \\ge {(-1)}^{3} \\\\ x+3 & \\ge -1 \\\\ x & \\ge -4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157138 1074 "Isolating the cubic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[3]{x + 3} + 1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x + 3} & \\ge - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[3]{x + 3})^{3} & \\ge (-1)^{3} \\\\ x + 3 & \\ge - 1 \\\\ x & \\ge - 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157139 1071 "Solve the inequality $3\\sqrt{x-2} +7 \\geq 4$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157139 1071 "Solve the inequality $3\\sqrt{x-2} +7 \\geq 4.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157139 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{x-2}+7 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2} & \\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx-2 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\geq 2.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157139 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{x - 2} + 7 & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge - 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x - 2} & \\ge - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1)$, so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx-2 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157140 1071 "Solve the inequality $7-\\sqrt{x+3} < 5$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157140 1071 "Solve the inequality $7-\\sqrt{x+3} < 5.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157140 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{x+3} & <5 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x+3} & <-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+3})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ x+3 & >4 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x > 1,$ which can be expressed as $\\left( 1,\\infty \\right).$ The domain of the square root is $x \\geq -3,$ which can be expressed as $\\left[ -3,\\infty \\right).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\left( 1,\\infty \\right) \\cap \\left[ -3,\\infty \\right)  =\\left( 1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in \\left( 1,\\infty\\right),$ which can also be written as $x > 1.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157140 1074 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 - \\sqrt{x + 3} & < 5 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x + 3} & < - 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x + 3} & > 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2)$. So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt{x + 3})^{2} & > 2^{2} \\\\ x + 3 & > 4 \\\\ x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x > 1$, which can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nThe domain of the square root is\n\n$$\nx \\geq -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\left[ -3,\\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nTo find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\infty \\right) \\cap \\left[ -3,\\infty \\right) =\\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as $x > 1$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157141 1071 "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 >  0.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157141 1071 "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 > 0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157141 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{x}-5 & >0 \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & >5 \\\\ {e}^{x} & >\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157141 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{x} - 5 & > 0 \\\\ 4e^{x} & > 5 \\\\ e^{x} & > \\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln (e^{x}) & > \\ln (\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & > \\ln 5 - \\ln 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157142 1071 "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x <   7^{3-x}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true]