uality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given inequality by $11^x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}((x^{2} - 8)^{3})/(11^{x}) & \\ge 0 \\\\ 11^{x} \\cdot ((x^{2} - 8)^{3})/(11^{x}) & \\ge 11^{x} \\cdot 0 \\\\ (x^{2} - 8)^{3} & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{(x^{2} - 8)^{3}}\\begin{vmatrix}\\ge \\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ x^{2} - 8\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 0; x^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\ge 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{x^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\ge \\sqrt{8}; \\mid x \\mid \\mid \\ge 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is\n\n$$\nx \\leq -2\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2 \\sqrt 2\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157145 1074 "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given inequality by $11^x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({x}^{2}-8)^{3}}{{11}^{x}} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {11}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{({x}^{2}-8)^{3}}{{11}^{x}} & \\ge {11}^{x}\\cdot 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-8)^{3} & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-8)^{3}} & \\ge \\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ {x}^{2}-8 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{8} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $x \\leq -2\\sqrt 2$ or $x \\geq 2 \\sqrt 2.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157146 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^2e^{x+1}-2e^{x+1} < -xe^{x+1} .$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157146 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^2e^{x+1}-2e^{x+1} < -xe^{x+1}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157146 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x+1}-2{e}^{x+1} & <-x{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {x}^{2}{e}^{x+1}+x{e}^{x+1}-2{e}^{x+1} & <0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x-2){e}^{x+1} & <0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1){e}^{x+1} & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $e^{x+1}$ and get\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-1) <   0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis when $-2 <   x <   1.$\n\n![](images/2f/2f01104ae0cb9a41c5c5e4b8393e346f7d0e9b2bfd1afa593ee6bdfb0fff39f2.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $-2 <   x <   1.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157146 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2}e^{x + 1} - 2e^{x + 1} & < - xe^{x + 1} \\\\ x^{2}e^{x + 1} + xe^{x + 1} - 2e^{x + 1} & < 0 \\\\ (x^{2} + x - 2)e^{x + 1} & < 0 \\\\ (x + 2)(x - 1)e^{x + 1} & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $e^{x+1}$ and get\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-1) < 0\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-2$ and $x=1$. The parabola is below the $x$-axis when $-2 < x < 1$.\n\n![](images/2f/2f01104ae0cb9a41c5c5e4b8393e346f7d0e9b2bfd1afa593ee6bdfb0fff39f2.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $-2 < x < 1$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157147 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^5 \\cdot 4^{x} > 4^{x - 15}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157147 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^5 \\cdot 4^{x} > 4^{x - 15}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157147 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}\\cdot {4}^{x} & >{4}^{x-15} \\\\ {x}^{5}\\cdot {4}^{x}-{4}^{x-15} & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{5}-{4}^{-15})\\cdot {4}^{x} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $4^{x}$ and get\n\n$$\nx^5 - 4^{-15} > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve this inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5} & >{4}^{-15} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{5}} & >\\sqrt[5]{{4}^{-15}} \\\\ x & >{({4}^{-15})}^{1/5} \\\\ x & >{4}^{-3} \\\\ x & >\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $x > \\dfrac{1}{64}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157147 1074 "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{5} \\cdot 4^{x} & > 4^{x - 15} \\\\ x^{5} \\cdot 4^{x} - 4^{x - 15} & > 0 \\\\ (x^{5} - 4^{-15}) \\cdot 4^{x} & > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $4^{x}$ and get\n\n$$\nx^5 - 4^{-15} > 0\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve this inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{5} & > 4^{-15} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{x^{5}} & > \\sqrt[5]{4^{-15}} \\\\ x & > (4^{-15})^{1/5} \\\\ x & > 4^{-3} \\\\ x & > \\frac{1}{64}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is\n\n$$\nx > \\dfrac{1}{64}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157148 1071 "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157148 1071 "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157148 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157148 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} + 2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} & \\ge 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln [(\\frac{3}{4})^{x}] & \\ge \\ln 2 \\\\ x\\ln (\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\ln 2 \\\\ x(\\ln 3 - \\ln 4) & \\ge \\ln 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\nx \\mid \\le \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 3 - \\ln 4}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157149 1071 "Solve the inequality $4 \\cdot 3^{8-x} + 12 > 0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157149 1071 "Solve the inequality $4 \\cdot 3^{8-x} + 12 > 0.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157149 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{8-x}+12 & >0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {3}^{8-x} & >-12 \\\\ {3}^{8-x} & >-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n3^{8-x} > 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $3^{8-x} > -3$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157149 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\cdot 3^{8 - x} + 12 & > 0 \\\\ 4 \\cdot 3^{8 - x} & > - 12 \\\\ 3^{8 - x} & > - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0$, we must have\n\n$$\n3^{8-x} > 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality $3^{8-x} > -3$ is satisfied for all real values of $x$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157172 1071 "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n2. $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n3. $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157172 1071 "Suppose $f(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(\\ln x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$\n\n- $f= \\mathcal O(xe^{x})$ as $x\\to\\infty$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157172 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty$. To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x$. Then, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{x}$.\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}$, we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{x}; = lim_(x → ∞)((e^{x} + x\\ln x)^{′})/((x)^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + \\ln x + 1}{1}; = 1 + lim_(x → ∞)(e^{x} + \\ln x); = 1 + ∞; = ∞$.\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x$. Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{\\ln x}; = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{\\ln x}; = lim_(x → ∞)((e^{x} + x\\ln x)^{′})/((\\ln x)^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + \\ln x + 1}{1/x}; = lim_(x → ∞)x(e^{x} + \\ln x + 1); = ∞$.\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}$. Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n$lim_(x → ∞)(f(x))/(g(x))|= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x} + x\\ln x}{xe^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{x} + lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}; = 0 + lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{e^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)((\\ln x)^{′})/((e^{x})^{′}); = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1/x}{e^{x}}; = lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{xe^{x}}; = 0$.\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157172 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathcal O(g(x)) \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty.$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x + x\\ln x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x) = x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =1+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x) =\\ln x.$  Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{1/x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f \\neq \\mathcal O(x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x) = xe^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f = \\mathcal O(xe^x)$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157176 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1}  = 0?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157176 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1} = 0$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157176 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}e^{x - 1} - 2xe^{x - 1} - 15e^{x - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 2x - 15)e^{x - 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15 = 0$ can be factored as\n$(x+3)(x-5)=0$,\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5$.\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x$.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5$, and their product is\n\n$$\n(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157176 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x-1}-2x{e}^{x-1}-15{e}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x-15){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15  = 0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157177 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157177 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157177 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x^{2} \\cdot 3^{x + 1} &= 16 \\cdot 3^{x + 1} \\\\\n9x^{2} \\cdot 3^{x + 1} - 16 \\cdot 3^{x + 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(9x^{2} - 16)3^{x + 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n$(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0$,\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}$.\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x$.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nand their product is\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157177 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1}-16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-16){3}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3},$ and their product is $\\left( -\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157179 1071 "![](images/ac/ac922b185c42645f703c13d4f5016941b4125d81e85fa5ff529aa5c8d4010ac8.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157179 1071 "![](images/ac/ac922b185c42645f703c13d4f5016941b4125d81e85fa5ff529aa5c8d4010ac8.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157179 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a)$. From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9$.\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny = \\pm 3 x\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a}{b} &= 3 \\\\\n\\frac{9}{b} &= 3 \\\\\nb &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157179 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are $y = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y = \\pm 3 x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{b} & =3 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157180 1071 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157180 1071 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157180 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157180 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{9} = 3$ and $b = \\sqrt{25} = 5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157181 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157181 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157181 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t$. We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n$x|= 2\\tan t; \\frac{x}{2}|= \\tan t; \\frac{x^{2}}{2^{2}}|= \\tan^{2} t; \\frac{x^{2}}{4}|= \\tan^{2} t$.\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n$y|= 3\\sec t; \\frac{y}{3}|= \\sec t; \\frac{y^{2}}{3^{2}}|= \\sec^{2} t; \\frac{y^{2}}{9}|= \\sec^{2} t$.\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}\n$$\n\nand rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} t + 1 &= \\sec^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + 1 &= \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{9} - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157181 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need 