\\frac{2}{5{x}^{4}+2} & <(5{x}^{4}+2)\\cdot \\frac{5-2x}{5{x}^{4}+2} \\\\ 2 & <5-2x \\\\ 2x & <3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157183 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157183 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157183 1074 "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}+3x & <0. \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{2}+1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{2}+1)}{2{x}^{2}+1} & <\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 3x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157183 1074 "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x^{3} + 3x & < 0. \\\\ 3x(2x^{2} + 1) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(3x(2x^{2} + 1))/(2x^{2} + 1) & < \\frac{0}{2x^{2} + 1} \\\\ 3x & < 0 \\\\ x & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157184 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157184 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157184 1074 "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2\\sin x - 9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ ((2\\sin x - 9)(2x))/(2\\sin x - 9) & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\sin x - 9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157184 1074 "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7.\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9)(2x)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9} & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157185 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+5}{1-3x} \\lt 2, \\quad x \\lt 0.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157185 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$\\dfrac{x+5}{1-3x} \\lt 2, \\quad x \\lt 0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157185 1074 "Since $x\\lt 0$, we have that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & < 0 \\\\ -x & > 0 \\\\ -3x & > 0 \\\\ 1 - 3x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0)$. So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x$, and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x + 5}{1 - 3x} & < 2 \\\\ (1 - 3x) \\cdot \\frac{x + 5}{1 - 3x} & < (1 - 3x) \\cdot 2 \\\\ x + 5 & < 2 - 6x \\\\ 7x & < - 3 \\\\ x & < - \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and\n\n$$\nx \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\nx \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157185 1074 "Since $x\\lt 0,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <0 \\\\ -x & >0 \\\\ -3x & >0 \\\\ 1-3x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <2 \\\\ (1-3x)\\cdot \\frac{x+5}{1-3x} & <(1-3x)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+5 & <2-6x \\\\ 7x & <-3 \\\\ x & <-\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157186 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(1,1)$\n\n- $(-4,4)$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157186 1071 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n1. $(0,0)$\n2. $(1,1)$\n3. $(-4,4)$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157186 1074 "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(0, 0)} &= \\left.(y + x)^{3}\\right|_{(0, 0)} \\\\\n&= (0 + 0)^{3} \\\\\n&= 0✓ \\\\\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(1, 1)} &= \\left.(y + x)^{3}\\right|_{(1, 1)} \\\\\n&= (1 + 1)^{3} \\\\\n&= 8 \\\\\n&\\ne 0\\cdot \\\\\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(-4, 4)} &= \\left.(y + x)^{3}\\right|_{(-4, 4)} \\\\\n&= (4 + (-4))^{3} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157186 1074 "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0+0)^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1+1)^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-4,4)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(-4,4)} \\\\  & =(4+(-4))^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157190 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}$.\n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157190 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157190 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4y - e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4y) - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{3x}) \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\ninto the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 4(4y - e^{3x}) - 3e^{3x} \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= 16y - 7e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}\\right|_{(0, 1)} &= \\left.(16y - 7e^{3x})\\right|_{(0, 1)} \\\\\n&= 16(1) - 7e^{3(0)} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157190 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y-{e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(4y-{e}^{3x})-3{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =16y-7{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(16y-7{e}^{3x})|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =16(1)-7{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157191 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y$.\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n3. $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157191 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157191 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0)$, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(2, 0)} &= \\left.(x^{2} - 4 - y)\\right|_{(2, 0)} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} - 4 - (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} - 4 - y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(4) - \\frac{d}{dx}(y) \\\\\n&= 2x - \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n&= 2x - (x^{2} - 4 - y) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} + 2x + 4 + y \\\\\n\\left.\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}}\\right|_{(2, 0)} &= \\left.(-x^{2} + 2x + 4 + y)\\right|_{(2, 0)} \\\\\n&= - (2)^{2} + 2(2) + 4 + (0) \\\\\n&= 4 \\\\\n&> 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0$,\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$.\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0)$. So, according to the second derivative test, since\n$\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} \\right|_{(2,0)} > 0$,\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157191 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,0)} & ={({x}^{2}-4-y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4-(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2x-({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(2,0)} & ={(-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+2(2)+4+(0) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}  \\right|_{(2,0)}  > 0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157197 1071 "![](images/29/2944e095d71b64fb2f9e6e0121a09fb9eb2f3ffe765f9881162c95fe6875a831.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157197 1071 "![](images/29/2944e095d71b64fb2f9e6e0121a09fb9eb2f3ffe765f9881162c95fe6875a831.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186157197 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1d78a939f85c533bcd9b4609346f2a15385aad3a6b074a62b791551cf784418.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right].\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186157197 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad t \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad t \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1d78a939f85c533bcd9b4609346f2a15385aad3a6b074a62b791551cf784418.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]\n$$\n\nwe get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right]\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161763 1071 "![](images/1d/1d7b89b6790