uadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms ($x_i x_j$ where\n\n$$\ni \\neq j\n$$\n\n). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2x_2x_3$.\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(\\mathbf{x})$ and $R(\\mathbf{x})$ only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161792 1074 "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms ($x_i x_j$ where $i \\neq j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2x_2x_3.$\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(\\mathbf{x})$ and $R(\\mathbf{x})$ only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161793 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-18x_1x_2+5x_2^2$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$4$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161793 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-18x_1x_2+5x_2^2$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n![](images/cc/cc77cb76fcab6bd72211e7e66d5fee5ba87c0ba1465571aa88baf26beb196a22.png)\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161793 1074 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and factor out the coefficient of $x_1^2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 9x_{1}^{2} - 18x_{1}x_{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (9x_{1}^{2} - 18x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9(x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9(x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} &= 9([x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}^{2}] - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9((x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9((x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} &= 9(x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - 9x_{2}^{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9(x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - 4x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x 1 - x 2; y_{2} = x_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $4 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_1-x_2$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161793 1074 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and factor out the coefficient of $x_1^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =9([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n![](images/e1/e1048614ed3dda333597bf32339f23a1585308e630233e3d11751c48c597ed52.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $4 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_1-x_2 .$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161794 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-4x_3^2+2x_1x_2-4x_1x_3+2x_2x_3$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n${y_{1} = [math]\\phantom{x_1+x_2-2x_3}[/math]; y_{2} = [math]\\phantom{x_2-3x_3}[/math]; y_{3} = x_{3}$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161794 1071 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-4x_3^2+2x_1x_2-4x_1x_3+2x_2x_3$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n![](images/bf/bf0297b0d758d02198d01565050bc5f3b02b0964336d3b84fdca331fabed6051.png)\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161794 1074 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{3}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - 4x_{1}x_{3} + 2x_{2}x_{3} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - 4x_{1}x_{3}) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}(x_{2} - 2x_{3})) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}(x_{2} - 2x_{3}) + (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2}) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 4x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 6x_{2}x_{3} - 8x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group $x_2^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_2$, factor out the coefficient of $x_2^2$, and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 6x_{2}x_{3} - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{2}x_{3}) - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - ([x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{2}x_{3} + 9x_{3}^{2}] - 9x_{3}^{2}) - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 3x_{3})^{2} + 9x_{3}^{2} - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 3x_{3})^{2} + x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x 1 + x 2 - 2 x 3; y_{2} = x 2 - 3 x 3; y_{3} = x_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x_1+x_2-2x_3 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_2-3x_3$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161794 1074 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}))+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2})+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group $x_2^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_2,$ factor out the coefficient of $x_2^2,$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3})-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-([{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+9{x}_{3}^{2}]-9{x}_{3}^{2})-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})^{2}+9{x}_{3}^{2}-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n![](images/84/84f88cbb41e573d7ed0793919e0ad25477a46637d09144ca9f462e12f0bc6e90.png)\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x_1+x_2-2x_3 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_2-3x_3.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161803 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+x_2^2+2x_3^2+4x_1x_2$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161803 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+x_2^2+2x_3^2+4x_1x_2.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161803 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/44/44753acffd8b6384cd291f9b0758edd7676acb319caa22d5b07be36dcf72a5a6.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/21/217b5d7d3b82800bdfc44d83ca8b068e02a37759e52b7a8382505bfb102f753f.png)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161803 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)((1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(λ^{2} - 2λ - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(λ - 3)(λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 3, 2,-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161804 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2$, find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161804 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2,$ find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161804 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/69/69dcf821f3ff202b567b901d8f512c5cecf7159617ca51a0664c14a5099e9715.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/fc/fc9465d4e5bcd43a3c29e5946b05ec18cdbc7453a4011aa548f374016c561b45.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/59/59c1bd46325fab8a52fed162d7ae47e8fb33a10cc524cd770a3b18f5934e51dd.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/ad/adc9241219f3b5ebce0d8418bf34e250b7230ea612fabca5bdfc109c98a18dbb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/dc/dcab437eb77b29ab5bcd5c3ae14b77f10f01382aafadf2aecded1bb1fb72dc32.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/3f/3f6b1002893290dde6c9a68425b0035e249ff3630a41d29c9da5756c1a18f6a6.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161804 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n