nd{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = 1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161805 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=4, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=2, \\quad$ and $\\quad\\lambda_3=1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/13/13eee082d48c56e6c7e796549eddc0185868b39841a7c5946ce9ce9e996ba553.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/1e/1e7c81e373377bd719e1bbc9e87a8c88d13f6116bcf2949897fc26246e2265d1.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned