2 + 8x_1x_2$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161806 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting ![](images/29/29545f79f3b34ca5395f98fa8c8c81e289482e866c3ad8c576aeb7b9fe8b8749.png)\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={2}^{2}-3\\cdot {7}^{2}+5\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =4-147+70 \\\\  & =-73<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =110>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={7}^{2}+8\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =49+112 \\\\  & =161>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161806 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed,\n$R(x)|= 2^{2} - 3 \\cdot 7^{2} + 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7; = 4 - 147 + 70; =-73 < 0$.\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n$S(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = 110 > 0$.\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n$T(x)|= 7^{2} + 8 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7; = 49 + 112; = 161 > 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161807 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/d1/d16c12fa9086e956b3b0516d42b40aaa121e3b40bd25a72c3d05d436ba410440.png) Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n\n- $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161807 1071 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161807 1074 "Given a quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwe have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A$, we obtain\n$\\mid A - λI \\mid |= 0;\\begin{vmatrix}-4 - λ & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix}|= 0; (-4 - λ)(-4 - λ) - 25|= 0; λ^{2} + 8λ - 9|= 0; (λ - 1)(λ + 9)|= 0; λ|= 1,-9$\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161807 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161808 1071 "![](images/d8/d81ce57017adec7daf2556322259f32ce4261e646dc8f1a48632beb085d9a9cb.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n\n- $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161808 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161808 1074 "Let $\\Delta_i$ for\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\ndenote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A$. Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$,\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$, we have $\\Delta_i < 0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6$. Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get\n$det(A)|=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; = (-6)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- (-5)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+ 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = (-6)(-2) + (5)(-7) + 12; =-11$.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n$Δ_{1}|= a_{11} =-6 < 0; Δ_{2}|= \\mid - 6\\begin{vmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}- 2|= 37 > 0; Δ_{3}|= \\mid A|=-11 < 0$\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$, and the matrix $A$, are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161808 1074 "Let $\\Delta_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$  denote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we have $\\Delta_i <          0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6.$ Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-5)|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)(-2)+(5)(-7)+12 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|=37>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-11<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x},$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161809 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2 = 20$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161809 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2 = 20.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161809 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 20\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\nBy solving the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\mid A-\\lambda I \\mid = 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=20 \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\lambda_2=10\n$$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=20$, we have\n$A - 20I|= \\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix} - 20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{10}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=10$, we have\n$A - 10I|= \\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix} - 10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{10}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161809 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2.$ The matrix of the form is \n\n![](images/31/31f8c54fecdb8b2e8b3f3cad20a642a39eab9ae2987d49849a1c1dabc34e5368.png)\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 20\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I| = 0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=20 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=10.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/51/51897cac20d424f599eba1c8aada5259279ab9ad263e7557d6614ce79e68fa0f.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](images/f9/f9913f9139af447be912bccc2350f086ce32bae89849495f8e82a3eebf8a7918.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/2c/2cdd8527be173b17e254bd4ecda571d71b15938c3a1f6cb2609122db2b10c64d.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](images/f6/f6764ef3200afce932740e8100915018b6a0c21e902d932b218e79bcd57e0b95.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/15/1549a73afb90f3fa0fb8218d9d63daa1a5534c3e546613cbf3f226371b8f1092.png) and ![](images/d4/d4a6e3f0c138e6dd83252df8adc14359ba67ae77dec0270264ea6d00c7b08667.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161810 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation $6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2= 70$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161810 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation $6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2= 70?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161810 1074 "Consider the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2\n$$\n\nTo determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}6 - λ & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 3λ - 70 &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 7,-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2$.\n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7y_{1}^{2} - 10y_{2}^{2} &= 70 \\\\\n\\frac{y_{1}^{2}}{10} - \\frac{y_{2}^{2}}{7} &= 1 \\\\\n(y_{1}^{2})/((\\sqrt{10})^{2}) - (y_{2}^{2})/((\\sqrt{7})^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161810 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2.$ To  determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ which is\n\n![](images/33/33a30f2c07875359a60caed74f802a2de29ac9e44089800ed8f30a2015303bce.png)\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}-10{y}_{2}^{2} & =70 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161821 1074 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation.\n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](images/bd/bd0d117543012be1c4eab0acda878c9aea67518e46c93048e8c249de1a0c03e6.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161821 1074 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](images/bd/bd0d117543012be1c4eab0acda878c9aea67518e46c93048e8c249de1a0c03e6.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161822 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\n- The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161822 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n2. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n3. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161822 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/f8/f8d153708f7872d0c96414a77c12379d40c6b60a5f08844945fb8a8f8c1809ab.png)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161822 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n$A = A^T$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\na_{ij} = a_{ji}\n$$\n\nfor all $i,j$. In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A$, we have\n$(A + A^{T})^{T}|= A^{T} + (A^{T})^{T}; = A^{T} + A; = A + A^{T}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n$AB|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161844 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/41/4114e6536f57106949ed39a6ae4979646a0dd58c5690d7e7bcf9ffed15de8086.png) and the quadratic curve $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=1,$ find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161844 1071 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1$. Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=1$, find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161844 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1\n$$\n\nare t