{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{x}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{x}^{\\infty }(1+x+y)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}(1+x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=x}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[(1+x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=x}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }0-(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{c}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{2}(1+2x)^{-1}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{4}{[\\frac{1}{1+2x}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{4}[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{4}=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=4.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161875 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\lt y \\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9c823d67872095514c6392038ff43497512de656e28b26160465760df0faa4ea.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0$, we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$. So the first condition is satisfied.\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have\n$\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\iint_{D} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\displaystyle \\int_{x}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{c}{(1+x+y)^3} \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1$.\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{∞}[\\int_{x}^{∞}(c)/((1 + x + y)^{3})dy]dx &= c\\int_{0}^{∞}[\\int_{x}^{∞}(1 + x + y)^{-3}dy]dx \\\\\n&= c\\int_{0}^{∞}[- \\frac{1}{2}(1 + x + y)^{-2}]_{y = x}^{y = ∞}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}\\int_{0}^{∞}[(1 + x + y)^{-2}]_{y = x}^{y = ∞}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}\\int_{0}^{∞}0 - (1 + 2x)^{-2}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{c}{2}\\int_{0}^{∞} - (1 + 2x)^{-2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{c}{2}\\int_{0}^{∞}(1 + 2x)^{-2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n> Then, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n> $\\frac{c}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}(1 + 2x)^{-2}dx|= \\frac{c}{2}[- \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 2x)^{-1}]_{0}^{∞}; =-\\frac{c}{4}[\\frac{1}{1 + 2x}]_{0}^{∞}; =-\\frac{c}{4}[0 - 1]; = \\frac{c}{4}$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{4}=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=4\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161876 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](images/f7/f7aed2445b7b518afecd13a5979a873b8bfc17e8eb1ff8d41f4a0d9c912deaf4.png)\n\nFind $P(X > 1, Y < 1).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161876 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{6},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, 0 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind $P(X > 1, Y < 1)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161876 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\\subset \\mathbb R^2$, then\n\n$$\nP\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\iint_A f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region\n\n$$\nD = [0,3]\\times [0,2]\n$$\n\nand the event\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{X > 1, Y < 1\\right\\}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to a subset of $D$, shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/b1/b1fc6548c909b9887c708c4c5dcce5b715d112defc7ce1fc639784bbaf55e111.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 1, Y < 1) &= ∬_{A}f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}∬_{A}dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}Area(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot (3 - 1) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161876 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ then\n\n$$\nP\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\iint_A f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D = [0,3]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A = \\left\\{ X > 1, Y < 1\\right\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/b1/b1fc6548c909b9887c708c4c5dcce5b715d112defc7ce1fc639784bbaf55e111.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1,Y<1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161877 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, find $P(X = 0)$.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161877 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(X = 0).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161877 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= f_{X}(x) = ∑_(y)f(x, y) \\\\\nP(Y = y) &= f_{Y}(y) = ∑_(x)f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{12}}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{12}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 0) &= f_{X}(0) \\\\\n&= f(0, 0) + f(0, 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161877 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{12}}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{12}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & ={f}_{X}(0) \\\\  & =f(0,0)+f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161878 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n