e{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =c{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] \\\\  & =c[(1-0)-(0-1)] \\\\  & =c[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161881 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0\\lt x\\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},0\\lt y \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/cc/ccb7cc52f8404dcca5d555d4fd81e6ae03e9689ccdd7c462fccbc2dc611db235.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0$, we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$. So the first condition is satisfied.\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}c\\sin (x+y) \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1$.\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}c\\sin (x + y)dy]dx|= c\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (x + y)dy]dx; = c\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}[- \\cos (x + y)]_{y = 0}^{y = π/2}dx; = c\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}(-\\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2}) + \\cos x)dx; = c\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos x - \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2})dx$\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n$c\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos x - \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{2})dx|= c[\\sin x - \\sin (x + \\frac{π}{2})]_{0}^{π/2}; = c[(\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) - \\sin π) - (\\sin (0) - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}))]; = c[(1 - 0) - (0 - 1)]; = c[1 - (-1)]; = 2c$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161900 1071 "![](images/76/765415456d7f33c3558e08e7c4fc01ef3e80961c1e073a210033d91741d78f60.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161900 1071 "![](images/76/765415456d7f33c3558e08e7c4fc01ef3e80961c1e073a210033d91741d78f60.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161900 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4$. Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\nL = πr = 4π\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161900 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4.$ Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=\\pi r=4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161901 1071 "![](images/84/84de7754ed9244e3d646e452f6ed6dab349c130d17eae1d4dbc94cb52bd761e1.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s$, where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161901 1071 "![](images/84/84de7754ed9244e3d646e452f6ed6dab349c130d17eae1d4dbc94cb52bd761e1.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161901 1074 "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/f4/f4e80a23910612bd55e7578a074f23c76535139889faff479c4dd0521e7496ff.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161901 1074 "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/f4/f4e80a23910612bd55e7578a074f23c76535139889faff479c4dd0521e7496ff.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s = \\displaystyle \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\displaystyle \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\displaystyle \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s$.\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C$, i.e.,\n$\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L$.\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161902 1071 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2$, defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}C_{1}:r_{1}(t)|= ⟨\\frac{1}{9}t^{3}, t⟩, & 0 \\le t \\le 3 \\\\ C_{2}:r_{2}(u)|= ⟨3u^{3}, 3u⟩, & 0 \\le u \\le 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k$. Which of the following must be true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161902 1071 "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\,t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\,3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186161902 1074 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n$x = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3$.\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = \\displaystyle \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186161902 1074 "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds  = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166591 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t$, and $y=e^t$, compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166591 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166591 1074 "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t$, where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} =-y\\sin (xy), \\frac{∂f}{∂y} =-x\\sin (xy)\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= -e^{t}\\sin (te^{t}), \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= -t\\sin (te^{t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 1, \\frac{dy}{dt} = e^{t}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dt} &= \\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{∂f}{∂y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} \\\\\n&= [- e^{t}\\sin (te^{t})] \\cdot 1 + [- t\\sin (te^{t})] \\cdot e^{t} \\\\\n&=-e^{t}\\sin (te^{t}) - te^{t}\\sin (te^{t}) \\\\\n&= - (1 + t)e^{t}\\sin (te^{t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166591 1074 "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166592 1071 "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x$, compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166592 1071 "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166592 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + y}}, \\frac{∂f}{∂y} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + y}}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} \\cdot (-\\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\sin x}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166592 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166593 1071 "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166593 1071 "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166593 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t$, where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = 2x, \\frac{∂f}{∂y} = 2y\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= 2s\\cos t, \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= 2t\\sin s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂x}{∂t} &= -s\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{∂y}{∂t} &= \\sin s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t} &= \\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t} + \\frac{∂f}{∂y} \\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\\n&= (2s\\cos t)(-s\\sin t) + (2t\\sin s)(\\sin s) \\\\\n&=-2s^{2}\\sin t\\cos t + 2t\\sin^{2} s \\\\\n&= 2t\\sin^{2} s - 2s^{2}\\sin t\\cos t \\\\\n&= 2t\\sin^{2} s - s^{2}\\sin 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t\n$$\n\nin the above simplification." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166593 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166594 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z}$, where $x = s + t$, $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166594 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z},$ where $x = s + t,$ $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166594 1074 "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t$, where\n\n$$\nx=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)\n$$\n\nand $z = z(s,t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} = ye^{xy + z}, \\frac{∂f}{∂y} = xe^{xy + z}, \\frac{∂f}{∂z} = e^{xy + z}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= (s - t)e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = (s - t)e^{-t^{2}}, \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= (s + t)e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = (s + t)e^{-t^{2}}, \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z} &= e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = e^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$, $y$, and $z$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂x}{∂t} &= 1, \\\\\n\\frac{∂y}{∂t} &= -1, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂t} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t} &= (s - t)e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot 1 + (s + t)e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot (-1) + e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= (s - t - s - t)e^{-t^{2}} \\\\\n&=-2te^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166594 1074 "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{