erefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\ln z+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = \\ln z + 1.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166708 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x + y.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186166708 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x + y$.\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x,y) = x+C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186166708 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 1 &= z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = z+1\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{z + 1}\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 1 \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{z + 1}dz &= \\intdx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid z + 1 \\mid &= x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\ln{\\mid x+y+1 \\mid} = x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = \\ln{\\mid