{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1)^{3/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{2/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171830 1071 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $5x^2 -10x - 4y - 7 = 0$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171830 1071 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $5x^2 -10x - 4y - 7 = 0.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171830 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x^{2} - 10x - 4y - 7 &= 0 \\\\\n5x^{2} - 10x &= 4y + 7 \\\\\n5[x^{2} - 2x] &= 4y + 7 \\\\\n5[(x - 1)^{2} - 1] &= 4y + 7 \\\\\n5(x - 1)^{2} - 5 &= 4y + 7 \\\\\n5(x - 1)^{2} &= 4y + 7 + 5 \\\\\n5(x - 1)^{2} &= 4y + 12 \\\\\n5(x - 1)^{2} &= 4(y + 3) \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= \\frac{4}{5}(y + 3) \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= 4(\\frac{1}{5})(y + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (1,-3)\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171830 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-10x-4y-7 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x & =4y+7 \\\\