s norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{13}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8$, we have\n$A - (-8)I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} + 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{13}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171839 1071 "![](images/96/9614ff63a66cf5428a4107828b08ba16c43e3e021f6620d408d8b70358a3e535.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/9a/9acec021ce321e77ad541ecbb0a38944aa4991ec26c721e0e59a521490269d06.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171839 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP$, where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171839 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/ef/efb691aec933aa631974add9dc9d7d122b03f5956b17459eb0e76751e07ea42a.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/8c/8c0ac2201e3e40bcc8e5e71c12413504694b201237069a4695fae94d33e7fcb4.png)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/14/1490159efc1ac2073ee029f918c136b7c16fd77b5f15e59334a3ae63e08a8c92.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171839 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$A=PDP^T$,\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A$.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=1\n$$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n$$\n\nComputing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + I &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2$. Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nDividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171843 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]$?\n\n1. $p: \\{0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n2. $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0, 1]$\n3. $r: [0,1] \\to [0, 1)$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171843 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]?$\n\n- $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\n- $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171843 1074 "The notation $f: X \\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y$. In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X$,\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $\\mathbb{R}$, the set of real numbers.\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[0, 1]$, the interval from $0$ and $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[0, 1)$, the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ but not including $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171843 1074 "The notation $f: X \\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $\\mathbb{R},$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[ 0, 1 ],$ the interval from $0$ and $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[ 0, 1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ but not including $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171844 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\n- $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\n- $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171844 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\n1. $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n2. $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n3. $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171844 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $\\mathbb Q$, and the codomain of $g$ is $Y$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171844 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $\\mathbb Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171845 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3$?\n\n1. $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n2. $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n3. $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171845 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\n- $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\n- $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171845 1074 "The notation $g(x)=x-x^3$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x-x^3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x \\mapsto x-x^3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171845 1074 "The notation $g(x)=x-x^3$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x-x^3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x \\mapsto x-x^3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171846 1071 "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\n- The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\n- The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171846 1071 "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{-1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$?\n\n1. The domain of $g$ is $\\{-1,0,1 \\}$\n2. The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n3. The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171846 1074 "The notation\n\n$$\ng: \\{-1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $\\{-1,0,1 \\}$ to the set $\\mathbb{R}$. In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $\\{-1,0,1 \\}$, and\n- the codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$. Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171846 1074 "The notation \n\n$$\ng: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$ to the set $\\mathbb{R}.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171847 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1)$, and $(-2,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(2, 1)} = 2(1) - 1 = 1 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(-2, 1)} = 2(1) - 1 = 1 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(-2, 2)} = 2(2) - 1 = 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/be/be6bc036e194683af09a5936d433d7d45c7532e06054d9d473f3b433167ea102.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171847 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=2(2)-1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/be/be6bc036e194683af09a5936d433d7d45c7532e06054d9d473f3b433167ea102.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171848 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y = -1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n\n- At every point along the line $x = 0,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n\n- At every point along the line $y = 0,$ the slope field is horizontal" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171848 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point along the line $y = -1$, the slope of the slope field does not vary\n2. At every point along the line $x = 0$, the slope of the slope field varies\n3. At every point along the line $y = 0$, the slope field is horizontal" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171848 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y$, the slope does not vary with $x$.\n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x$.\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1$,\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y$.\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f3/f3ca77530d9126d733d33dc01ccee82d2740f789dcf712c67f03139fb09b753b.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171848 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f3/f3ca77530d9126d733d33dc01ccee82d2740f789dcf712c67f03139fb09b753b.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171849 1071 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)$?\n\n![](images/39/3990c49349d446512d59f1f722506ea7337143eba6ec49f29dc87af060fb553b.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171849 1071 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)?$ \n\n![](images/39/3990c49349d446512d59f1f722506ea7337143eba6ec49f29dc87af060fb553b.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171849 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y$, the slope does not vary with $x$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/df/df3dd6c7db3e88ac2ac2a0ad82b19e5cac427f69ddf0eeba841445612e62ec4e.png)\n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](images/4d/4d518885f30e40ed870dba7b902139fca6ccbde81f7e468631c5199e887c4923.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171849 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/df/df3dd6c7db3e88ac2ac2a0ad82b19e5cac427f69ddf0eeba841445612e62ec4e.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](images/4d/4d518885f30e40ed870dba7b902139fca6ccbde81f7e468631c5199e887c4923.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171850 1071 "For the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y,\n$$\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171850 1071 "For the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y$,\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,2)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171850 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(1, 1)} &= 2(1)^{2} - 1 = 1 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(0, 1)}|= 2(0)^{2} - 1 =-1 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(1, 2)}|= 2(1)^{2} - 2 = 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/0c/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171850 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & =2(1)^{2}-1=1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & =2(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)^{2}-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/0c/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171851 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171851 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n2. At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n3. At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171851 1074 "First, we define\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = -2xy\n$$\n\nThen, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 0\n$$\n\nwhen $x=0$ or $y=0$. The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y) \\lt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171851 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y) = -2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171852 1071 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y$?\n\n![](images/3b/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171852 1071 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171852 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y$. This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/43/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171852 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/43/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171853 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171853 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n2. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n3. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171853 1074 "First, we define\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1)\n$$\n\nThen, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 0\n$$\n\nwhen $x = -1$ or $y = -1$. This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7b398c7ff971e39b15980078232556d23866ab2f65a05b7d8df9a5f67b797dc.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/94/943ed7c12c71438d567da5ec901435bae1d3c7e576ba9b6c933d9fddc4e42b8f.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\lt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171853 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x = -1$ or $y = -1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7b398c7ff971e39b15980078232556d23866ab2f65a05b7d8df9a5f67b797dc.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/94/943ed7c12c71438d567da5ec901435bae1d3c7e576ba9b6c933d9fddc4e42b8f.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171856 1071 "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171856 1071 "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{rad/s}$. Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0$, find the position function of the particle." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171856 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O$, and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0$.\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0)$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i\n$$\n\nand we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7$.\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and\n\n$$\n\\omega= -3 \\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\n(negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= 7(\\cos (-3t)i + \\sin (-3t)j) \\\\\n&= 7(\\cos 3ti - \\sin 3tj)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171856 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0),$ we have that $\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and $\\omega= -3  \\textrm{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3t)\\,j) \\\\  & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171857 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171857 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171857 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= (r(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (5\\cos πti + 5\\sin πtj)^{′} \\\\\n&=-5π\\sin πti + 5π\\cos πtj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid v(t) \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-5π\\sin πt)^{2} + (5π\\cos πt)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25π^{2}\\sin^{2} πt + 25π^{2}\\cos^{2} πt} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25π^{2}(\\sin^{2} πt + \\cos^{2} πt)} \\\\\n&= 5π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{m/s}$.\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid = R \\mid \\omega \\mid\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nR=5\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand the angular frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid = 5\\cdot \\mid \\pi \\mid = 5\\pi \\textrm{m/s}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171857 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)^{2}+(5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+25{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t)} \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = R|\\omega|,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = 5\\cdot |\\pi| = 5\\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171858 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\,  \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171858 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\, \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171858 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= (r(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (4(\\cos 3ti + \\sin 3tj))^{′} \\\\\n&=-12\\sin 3ti + 12\\cos 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= (v(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (-12\\sin 3ti + 12\\cos 3tj)^{′} \\\\\n&=-36\\cos 3ti - 36\\sin 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid a(t) \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-36\\cos 3t)^{2} + (-36\\sin 3t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2}\\cos^{2} 3t + 36^{2}\\sin^{2} 3t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2}} \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nNote that $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad \\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid = \\omega^2 \\mid \\mid \\mathbf r \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\na=4\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand the angular frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid = 3^2\\cdot \\mid 4 \\mid = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171858 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{36}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}} \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nNote that $||\\mathbf a||$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad ||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2||\\mathbf r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = 3^2\\cdot |4| = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171859 1074 "A simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nSo, a curve that intersects itself somewhere between its endpoints is not a simple curve. Among the given options, the only that is not a simple curve is the following:\n\n \n\n![](images/0f/0fa8f32c9e9e98b668fabd032754bc0d13365c422f73a3b719070cd7492814cb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct option is diagram I." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171859 1074 "A simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nSo, a curve that intersects itself somewhere between its endpoints is not a simple curve. Among the given options, the only that is not a simple curve is the following:\n\n![](images/0f/0fa8f32c9e9e98b668fabd032754bc0d13365c422f73a3b719070cd7492814cb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct option is diagram I." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171860 1074 "A curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point. So, a curve whose terminal point does not coincide with its initial point is not closed.\n\nOf the given options, the only one that is not a closed curve is the following:\n\n![](images/da/da0561c80eac9f87194c19c321b052902ef86b4d90437c5725d7729b719407ce.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct option is diagram III." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171860 1074 "A curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point. So, a curve whose terminal point does not coincide with its initial point is not closed. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is not a closed curve is the following:\n\n![](images/da/da0561c80eac9f87194c19c321b052902ef86b4d90437c5725d7729b719407ce.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct option is diagram III." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171861 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented.\n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not simple.\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n- Curve III is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n- Curve IV is neither simple nor closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171861 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is not simple.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve IV is neither simple nor closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171862 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D$.\n\nAmong the given options, only regions I, II, and III satisfy this definition.\n\nRegion IV does not satisfy this definition. Note that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n![](images/d4/d4d61cf87f2c62c5cf4727384d6dfcc05cada6b23025dd6dd674130eaf64dd45.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171862 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, only regions I, II, and III satisfy this definition.\n\nRegion IV does not satisfy this definition. Note that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n![](images/d4/d4d61cf87f2c62c5cf4727384d6dfcc05cada6b23025dd6dd674130eaf64dd45.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171863 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D$. In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:\n\n- Region I is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n- Region III is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n- Region IV is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171863 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions: \n\n- Region I is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\n- Region IV is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171866 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =  \\left\\langle ye^{xy},\\, xe^{xy}\\right\\rangle$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_1$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_2$ is the path along along the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171866 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\left\\langle ye^{xy},\\, xe^{xy}\\right\\rangle$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e$, where $C_1$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}$, traversed from $A$ to $B$.\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e$, where $C_2$ is the path along along the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, traversed from $A$ to $B$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171866 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B$, and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = (1+xy)e^{xy}$.\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F$. Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n$f = e^{xy}$.\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr|= \\displaystyle \\int_(C_{1})∇f \\cdot dr; = f(2, 2) - f(1, 1); = e^{2 \\cdot 2} - e^{1 \\cdot 1}; = e^{4} - e$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative and $C_2$ has the same initial and terminal points as $C_1$, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^{4}-e$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171866 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = (1+xy)e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\cdot 2}-{e}^{1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative and $C_2$ has the same initial and terminal points as $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^{4}-e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171867 1071 "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=x^2$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right]$. What is $\\mathbf{r}$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186171867 1071 "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=x^2$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right].$ What is $\\mathbf{r}?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186171867 1074 "![](images/56/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in[a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x) = x^2 \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in \\left[0,1\\right].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, 