17592186045585 true] [17592186175838 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= - (x - y)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n$z = x - y$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - \\frac{dz}{dx} &= -z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = z^2 + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(z) = z^2 + 1\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175838 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(x-y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x - y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  =  z^2 + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = z^2 + 1.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175839 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{1 - x - y}{x + y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = 2x + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175839 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1 - x - y}{x + y}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x,y) = 2x + C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175839 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (1 - (x + y))/(x + y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 1 &= \\frac{1 - z}{z}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form\n\n$$\nz'(x) = f(z)\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{z}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 1 \\\\\n\\intzdz &= \\intdx \\\\\n\\frac{z^{2}}{2} &= x + K \\\\\nz^{2} &= 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 2K$.\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 = 2x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = (x+y)^2\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175839 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-(x+y)}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{1-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =x+K \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 = 2x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y) = (x+y)^2.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175840 1071 "The second-order differential equation\n$y'' - 7t y' + e^{t}y= -t$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = f(t)$\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$ and $x_2(t) = y'(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175840 1071 "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\ny''  - 7t y' + e^{t}y= -t\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$ and $x_2(t) = y'(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175840 1074 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= y \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′}(t) &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′}(t) &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = 7tx_{2} - e^{t}x_{1} - t\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175840 1074 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=7t{x}_{2}-{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175844 1071 "![](images/ff/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown 