\\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)dA &= ∬_(Δ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/2}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{3}rdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175844 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175845 1071 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175845 1071 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](images/1a/1a6c3918b93419f9576951374ebd69b2df271106345493084ffa0797b0a35847.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175845 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J$, we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}5 - λ & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(5 - λ)(-2 - λ) - 4 \\cdot 2 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 3λ - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 6)(λ + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=6\n$$\n\nSince we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note**: A Jordan canonical form is unique up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. Therefore, another Jordan canonical form for this matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175845 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda