For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = 2y+2x, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\cos{x} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d609a1288446f0f3adc0de65b814f2a6daaf2d398478e7834882f3be68c8c.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y + 2x) = 2, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y + 2x) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (2)^{2} + (2)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3dA \\\\\n&= 3\\int_{0}^{π/2}\\int_{0}^{\\cos x}dydx \\\\\n&= \\left.3\\int_{0}^{π/2}y\\right|_{0}^{\\cos x}dx \\\\\n&= 3\\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.3\\sin x\\right|_{0}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= 3(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175854 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = 2y+2x, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\cos{x} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d609a1288446f0f3adc0de65b814f2a6daaf2d398478e7834882f3be68c8c.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+2x)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+2x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3(1-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175855 1071 "![](images/6c/6c6a46e8ca4ea71ef0bff620efff6d35ec43e3f20d05ffe3ad8b01c657f94f85.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a+b.$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175855 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $a+b$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175855 1074 "Since the Jordan block $J$ has a single $3 \\times 3$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda=-4$, the columns of $P$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\nv_{3}→^(A + 4 I)v_{2}→^(A + 4 I)v_{1}→^(A + 4 I)0\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that\n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4$,\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ such that $(A+4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1$,\n- the third column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ such that $(A+4I)\\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{v}_2$.\n\nNow, computing $A+4I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + 4I &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4\\en