 of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J$, we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(-4 - λ) - 3 \\cdot (-3) &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)(λ + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175867 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](images/c8/c8716d15efb054f488535b139e201dda7bc61a3fb8880caf50d65bee515c2cc1.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175868 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175868 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](images/4b/4b66afe6c1771afad4987215e3ddc02865a9f873ae93050f940c36bc3ff86356.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175868 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175868 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda = -3,$ and\n\n![](images/2b/2be33154f6a1933a12a3c43474464edb9c4e3fd2b2a4213d789b49fc1e4aec77.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175869 1071 "![](images/54/54ae1bc6614a1fe70fac039fbdd3defd2aeeceb3648679ee3d4c9ad10f6f15b5.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/a9/a985e5861e7360654b98ca334fac6d55f435e271dcc52b313e350f74a4c755c5.png)" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186175869 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186175869 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J = J_2(1)$.\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\nJ_{2}(1) \\mid: \\mid v_{2}→^(A - 1 I)v_{1}→^(A - 1 I)0\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda=1$.\n\nNow, computing $A-1I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 1I &= \\begin{bma