\,\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179554 1071 "Calculate $\\nabla\\times(3\\mathbf{F} + \\mathbf{G})$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ for the vector fields $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G,$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = 3x\\,\\mathbf{i} + y^2z\\,\\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}, \\quad (\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{G})(x,y,z) = 3y^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4z\\,\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179554 1074 "If $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $\\nabla \\times (k \\mathbf F) = k \\nabla \\times \\mathbf F$\n\n- $\\nabla \\times (\\mathbf F + \\mathbf G) = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf F + \\nabla \\times \\mathbf G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (3F+G) & =∇\\times (3F)+∇\\times G \\\\  & =3∇\\times F+∇\\times G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3x & {y}^{2}z & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}z)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3x)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}z)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)]k \\\\  & =(0-{y}^{2})i-(0-0)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (3F+G) & =3∇\\times F+∇\\times G \\\\  & =3[-{y}^{2}\\,i]+[3{y}^{2}\\,i+4z\\,j+k] \\\\  & =-3{y}^{2}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,i+4z\\,j+k \\\\  & =(3{y}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+4z\\,j+k \\\\  & =4z\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(-1,1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\times (F-G))(-1,1,0) & =4(0)\\,j+k=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179554 1074 "If $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $\\nabla \\times (k \\mathbf F) = k \\nabla \\times \\mathbf F$\n- $\\nabla \\times (\\mathbf F + \\mathbf G) = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf F + \\nabla \\times \\mathbf G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (3F + G) &= ∇ \\cdot (3F) + ∇ \\cdot G \\\\\n&= 3∇ \\cdot F + ∇ \\cdot G\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}$. In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3x & y^{2}z & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y^{2}z)]i - [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(3x)]j + [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2}z) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)]k \\\\\n&= (0 - y^{2})i - (0 - 0)j + (0 - 0)k \\\\\n&=-y^{2}i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (3F + G) &= 3∇ \\cdot F + ∇ \\cdot G \\\\\n&= 3[- y^{2}i] + [3y^{2}i + 4zj + k] \\\\\n&=-3y^{2}i + 3y^{2}i + 4zj + k \\\\\n&= (3y^{2} - 3y^{2})i + 4zj + k \\\\\n&= 4zj + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(-1,1,0)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∇ \\cdot (F - G))(-1, 1, 0) &= 4(0)j + k = k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179555 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac 1x, xy \\right \\rangle$ and $f(x,y) = \\dfrac 1{x}$, calculate $\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179555 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac 1x, xy \\right \\rangle$ and $f(x,y) = \\dfrac 1{x},$ calculate $\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179555 1074 "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F) = f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}+  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}$ and $\\nabla f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},0⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+x)+⟨\\frac{1}{x},xy⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179555 1074 "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F) = f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}+ \\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}$ and $\\nabla f$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f &= ⟨ - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}, 0⟩, \\\\\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) =-\\frac{1}{x^{2}} + x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (fF) &= f∇ \\cdot F + F \\cdot ∇f \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x}(-\\frac{1}{x^{2}} + x) + ⟨\\frac{1}{x}, xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}, 0⟩ \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{3}} + 1 - \\frac{1}{x^{3}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179556 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) =  \\langle 0, y-z, x-y\\rangle$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x+y.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times \\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\,-1,\\,0\\rangle, \n\\quad\n\\nabla f = \\langle 1,\\, 1,\\, 0\\rangle,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F)$ at the point $(-1,1,3).$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179556 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0, y-z, x-y\\rangle$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x+y$. Given that\n$\\nabla\\times \\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\,-1,\\,0\\rangle, \\quad \\nabla f = \\langle 1,\\, 1,\\, 0\\rangle$,\ncalculate $\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F)$ at the point $(-1,1,3)$." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179556 1074 "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F) = f \\nabla \\times \\mathbf {F}+   \\nabla f \\times \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla f \\times \\mathbf F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & y-z & x-y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[1\\cdot (x-y)-0\\cdot (y-z)]\\,i-[1\\cdot (x-y)-0\\cdot (0)]\\,j+[1\\cdot (y-z)-1\\cdot (0)]\\,k \\\\  & =(x-y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j+(y-z)\\,k \\\\  & =⟨x-y,\\,y-x,\\,y-z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(x+y)⟨0,-1,0⟩+⟨x-y,\\,y-x,\\,y-z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-(x+y),0⟩+⟨x-y,\\,y-x,\\,y-z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x-y,\\,-2x,\\,y-z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(-1,1,3) & =⟨(-1)-1,\\,-2(-1),\\,1-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179556 1074 "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times(f\\,\\mathbf F) = f \\nabla \\times \\mathbf {F}+ \\nabla f \\times \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla f \\times \\mathbf F$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f \\cdot F &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & y - z & x - y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= [1 \\cdot (x - y) - 0 \\cdot (y - z)]i - [1 \\cdot (x - y) - 0 \\cdot (0)]j + [1 \\cdot (y - z) - 1 \\cdot (0)]k \\\\\n&= (x - y)i + (y - x)j + (y - z)k \\\\\n&= ⟨x - y, y - x, y - z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (fF) &= f∇ \\cdot F + ∇f \\cdot F \\\\\n&= (x + y)⟨0,-1, 0⟩ + ⟨x - y, y - x, y - z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, - (x + y), 0⟩ + ⟨x - y, y - x, y - z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨x - y,-2x, y - z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot (fF)(-1, 1, 3) &= ⟨(-1) - 1,-2(-1), 1 - 3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 2, 2,-2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179562 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 301\n$$\n\nthousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{301}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that\n\n$$\nM(t) = 0.65 M_0\n$$\n\nSo, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.65M_0 = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.65M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n0.65M_{0} &= M_{0}e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n0.65 &= e^{-t\\ln 2/301} \\\\\n\\ln (0.65) &= \\ln (e^{-t\\ln 2/301}) \\\\\n\\ln (0.65) &= -\\frac{t\\ln 2}{301} \\\\\nt &= - (301 \\cdot \\ln (0.65))/(\\ln 2) \\\\\n&= t \\mid \\approx 187\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $187$ thousand years old." 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179562 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 301$ thousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{301}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 0.65 M_0.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.65M_0 = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.65{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.65\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.65 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{301} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{301\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈187.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $187$ thousand years old." 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179563 1071 "The mass $M(t)$, in grams, of a sample of iron-60 is given by\n\n$M(t) = 100 e^{-kt}$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. The half-life of iron-60 is $2.6$ million years. Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k$?" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179563 1071 "The mass $M(t),$ in grams, of a sample of iron-60 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t) = 100 e^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. The half-life of iron-60 is $2.6$ million years. Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k?$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179563 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\n$$\n\nSince we know that\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = 2.6\n$$\n\nmillion years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2.6} \\approx 0.267\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179563 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 2.6$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2.6} \\approx 0.267.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179569 1071 "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2$. What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y < -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x< 3$ | $x> 3$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 false] [17592186179569 1071 "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<   3$ | $x> 3$ |" 13194139533390 17592186045585 true] [17592186179569 1074 "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1$. These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df8519853487a7da738b4b3c477b141e00287c67dcb9eb804cbd385c6f43b611.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/46/4666739c474f85cbf8de7e7ca183206ab7b0bd804816a5504f9c1d97b03956b4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/e7/e789ce41c696b456e0c9d79ba8ce60aba030e454ab614902ea13c8d9ccd02d8c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y < -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  |